      "source": "Textbook",
      "note": "",
      "text": "After World War I, Britain, France, and the United States were major world powers. The United States was the leading economic power of the 1920s, but when things went wrong in the U.S, the whole world was affected. In 1929, the American stock market crashed. Banks failed, businesses closed and people lost money and jobs. As a result, foreign trade almost stopped. Soon the U.S. Great Depression of the 1930s began Worldwide depression leaving millions out of work. Across Europe, people lost faith in their governments, including their democratic governments. In France and Britain, democracy survived. In other nations, hopeless people turned to leaders who made impossible promises in exchange for absolute power. A **dictator** is a ruler who has complete control over their country.",
